<%# The AI hub (doc/ki_bereich_navigation_plan.md §3.2; the row list since
    doc/ki_uebersicht_zeilenliste_plan.md).

    "Der Hub ist kein Verteiler, sondern die Antwort": the page answers "muss ich etwas
    tun?" in three places and no more. The sentence at the top says whether AI runs.
    The one main action in the title row is the first open step of the setup, in the
    order the Zugang plan (§3) intends: choose the access, throw the switch — when both
    are done there is no button. The rows carry a dot only where something needs
    doing. It must not dramatise — its value lies in being boring until it is not.

    Two faces (§3.2): with AI switched off the rows stay VISIBLE but locked. Hiding them
    would be worse — a prospect could then not see what they would get, and that scope
    is what the product sells. "Zugang" stays reachable either way, because preparing
    the access before switching on is the intended order and locking that row would
    block it (D3 of the Zugang plan); "Protokoll" too, because the trail keeps its
    value after an organisation stops using AI. %>
<div>
<% ai_on = @organisation.ai_enabled? %>

<%# The main action: one lime button, or none. Access before switch, because choosing
    who processes the content is the step that carries legal weight. %>
<% title_action = capture do %>
  <% if !@access_chosen %>
    <%= render Neo::Buttons::ButtonComponent.new(
      variant: :primary, size: :medium,
      url: organisation_ai_settings_path(@organisation)
    ) do %>
      <%= t('views.organisations.ai_overview.actions.set_up_access') %>
    <% end %>
  <% elsif !ai_on %>
    <%= render Neo::Buttons::ButtonComponent.new(
      variant: :primary, size: :medium,
      confirm: t('views.confirm.ai_activate'),
      form: { action: activate_organisation_ai_settings_path(@organisation), method: :post }
    ) do %>
      <%= t('views.organisations.ai_overview.actions.switch_on') %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
<%= render 'organisations/shared/settings_subnav', active: :ai, title_action: title_action %>

<%= render 'organisations/ai_overviews/status' %>

<%# Capped (layout.css, "Page widths"; Nachtrag 3 of the plan): directories are
    capped, tables are not. The ticket list may take the full width because every
    column there is data one compares; here are five ways with one state each, and
    more width brings no information, only distance between a name and its value.
    The value column ends on the cap, a visible second edge. The sentence above is
    text and stays at 70ch. %>
<ul id="ai_overview_rows" class="width-capped" role="list">
  <%# ── Zugang ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Value: who processes — the package, the org's own credentials, or nothing chosen.
      Description: the three things that answer "who processes our content, where".

      The dot: red when the last connection check failed, amber when nothing is chosen,
      nothing otherwise — and nothing at all while AI is off, because nothing is missing
      while nothing runs. Red outranks amber: a failed access is the one reason to act,
      and a switch running on an access that does not answer is going to fail anyway.
      "Nie geprüft" stays dotless: it is the absence of a verdict, not a third verdict.

      Z3 (doc/ki_zugang_umbau_plan.md D7): no package and no credentials reads as
      "nicht eingerichtet", never as "Eigene Konfiguration" — a blank package id means
      custom by convention (E1-C), which turned "nothing is set up" into a claim that
      something was. Same rule, same owner as the access page. %>
  <% switch_running = @switch_progress.present? && @switch_progress.in_progress? %>
  <% access_state, access_state_title =
       if ai_on && @organisation.ai_connection_failed?
         [:alert, t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.state_failed')]
       elsif ai_on && !@access_chosen
         [:attention, t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.state_missing')]
       end %>
  <%= render 'organisations/ai_overviews/row',
        path: organisation_ai_settings_path(@organisation),
        locked: false,
        title: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.title'),
        value: if @package
                 @package.name
               elsif @organisation.ai_custom_access_configured?
                 t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.value_custom')
               else
                 t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.value_none')
               end,
        state: access_state,
        state_title: access_state_title,
        description: if @organisation.ai_connection_failed?
                       # ⚠️ :long, NOT :short — `time.formats.short` carries no year, and a
                       # failed check from 2024 would read like this afternoon on the one
                       # row whose whole job is to say "this is broken, and since when".
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.description_check_failed',
                         date: l(@organisation.ai_connection_checked_at, format: :long))
                     elsif switch_running
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.description_switching')
                     elsif @package
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.description_package',
                         jurisdiction: t("views.organisations.ai_settings.packages.jurisdictions.#{@package.effective_jurisdiction.presence || 'blank'}"),
                         tier: @tier&.label.presence || t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.no_tier'))
                     elsif @organisation.ai_custom_access_configured?
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.description_custom')
                     else
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.access.description_none')
                     end %>

  <%# ── Verbrauch ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Credits, not tokens (L8): tokens are our purchasing unit, credits are what the
      customer is held to. Beneath the value the 4 px bar shows the used share of the
      budget; the dot says when the share is a problem: red once the hard limit blocks
      new calls, amber in the grace zone or from 80 % on. %>
  <% usage_state, usage_state_title =
       if !ai_on
         nil
       elsif @quota.hard_exhausted?
         [:alert, t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.usage.state_exhausted')]
       elsif @quota.in_grace? || @quota.usage_percent >= 80
         [:attention, t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.usage.state_nearly')]
       end %>
  <%= render 'organisations/ai_overviews/row',
        path: organisation_ai_usage_path(@organisation),
        locked: !ai_on,
        title: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.usage.title'),
        value: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.usage.value_html',
                 used: ai_overview_figure(number_with_delimiter(@quota.used)),
                 budget: ai_overview_figure(number_with_delimiter(@quota.budget))),
        bar_percent: @quota.usage_percent,
        state: usage_state,
        state_title: usage_state_title,
        description: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.usage.description') %>

  <%# ── Indexierung ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      "Sync Status" was implementation language; what the admin wants to know is
      whether their content is searchable. Figures come from Embeddings::IndexHealth
      (ONE query, §3.3) and never from AiSyncStatusService.

      The value names failures first, the backlog second, and "nichts ausstehend" when
      both are zero: a running indexing is the normal case and gets no colour, only
      failures do. paused (AI off) is asked first for the description, same order and
      same reason as the Indexierung summary: with the opt-in off the figure is not a
      backlog anybody owes, and the row used to say "Verarbeitung läuft" over it
      indefinitely. %>
  <% index_state, index_state_title =
       if ai_on && @index_health.failed.positive?
         [:alert, t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.state_failed')]
       end %>
  <%= render 'organisations/ai_overviews/row',
        path: organisation_ai_sync_status_path(@organisation),
        locked: !ai_on,
        title: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.title'),
        value: if @index_health.failed.positive?
                 t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.value_failed_html',
                   failed: ai_overview_figure(number_with_delimiter(@index_health.failed)))
               elsif @index_health.pending.positive?
                 t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.value_pending_html',
                   pending: ai_overview_figure(number_with_delimiter(@index_health.pending)))
               else
                 t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.value_clear')
               end,
        state: index_state,
        state_title: index_state_title,
        description: if @index_health.paused?
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.description_paused')
                     elsif @index_health.failed.positive? && @index_health.pending.positive?
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.description_failed_pending',
                         pending: number_with_delimiter(@index_health.pending))
                     elsif @index_health.failed.positive?
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.description_failed')
                     elsif @index_health.pending.positive?
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.description_pending')
                     else
                       t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.index.description_clear')
                     end %>

  <%# ── Chat-Personas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Purely informative (§3.2) — no dot, because there is no bad number here. %>
  <%= render 'organisations/ai_overviews/row',
        path: organisation_prompt_personas_path(@organisation),
        locked: !ai_on,
        title: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.personas.title'),
        value: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.personas.value_html',
                 count: @persona_count,
                 number: ai_overview_figure(number_with_delimiter(@persona_count))),
        description: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.personas.description') %>

  <%# ── Protokoll ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      NOT locked while AI is off. Every other locked row leads somewhere that only
      means something with AI on; this one leads to the record of the switch that was
      just thrown. Locking it would hide the evidence of a deactivation at exactly the
      moment somebody goes looking for it.

      The value is the trail's first entry (AiOverviewsController#protocol_since), and
      it is the one audit figure this page keeps: the sentence at the top no longer
      names who switched on and when, this row says where that is written. %>
  <%= render 'organisations/ai_overviews/row',
        path: organisation_ai_protocol_path(@organisation),
        locked: false,
        title: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.protocol.title'),
        value: if @protocol_since
                 t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.protocol.value_since_html',
                   date: ai_overview_figure(l(@protocol_since.to_date)))
               else
                 t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.protocol.value_empty')
               end,
        description: t('views.organisations.ai_overview.rows.protocol.description') %>
</ul>
</div>
